Licence and sources

1. Lexifina's own work

CC0 1.0 Universal

The person who associated a work with this deed has dedicated the work to the public domain by waiving all of his or
her rights to the work worldwide under copyright law, including all related and neighboring rights, to the extent
allowed by law.

You can copy, modify, distribute and perform the work, even for commercial purposes, all without asking permission.

Full legal code: https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

This dedication covers Lexifina's own work: the history as commits, the commit messages, the folder layout, the
indexes and tables, the tools and the documentation. It does not cover the texts and records listed in section 2,
which come from their publishers and keep whatever terms those publishers give them.

2. Texts and records from their publishers

"Gesetze, Verordnungen, amtliche Erlasse und Bekanntmachungen sowie Entscheidungen und amtlich verfasste Leitsätze zu
Entscheidungen genießen keinen urheberrechtlichen Schutz." (§ 5 Abs. 1 UrhG.) The same holds for other official works
published in the official interest for general information, which must not be altered and must name their source
(§ 5 Abs. 2 UrhG). Most of this repository is such text.
Only the official promulgation (the Bundesgesetzblatt, a Land's gazette, the decision as given by its court) is
authoritative. Publishers' own material (editorial headnotes, summaries, annotations, footnotes) is left out. Where a
source states further terms, they are given below; where it states none, that is said. Some folders imported from
Lexifina's earlier repositories carry their own LICENSE or NOTICE file for that part; section 2.3 adds what has been
added to those folders since.

2.1 Federal law and the Grundgesetz (grundgesetz/, bund/gesetze/)
    Gesetze im Internet (www.gesetze-im-internet.de), Bundesministerium der Justiz: the official consolidated text
    (not authoritative; only the Bundesgesetzblatt is), official works under § 5 UrhG.
    The versions since 10 June 2019 come from QuantLaw's archive of Gesetze im Internet
    (https://github.com/QuantLaw/gesetze-im-internet), licensed BSD-3-Clause; the texts in it are official works.
    The Grundgesetz to the 63rd amending law comes from DocPatch (https://github.com/c3e/grundgesetz-dev). DocPatch
    says the Grundgesetz is an official work under § 5 UrhG and that its own content ("Selbst erarbeitete Inhalte",
    such as its metadata of dates, signatories, Gazette citations and votes) is licensed CC BY-SA 3.0 DE
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/). Credit: DocPatch, c3e/grundgesetz-dev.
    The dates of the 64th to 69th amending laws are facts taken from the Deutscher Bundestag's Datenhandbuch.

2.2 Bundesgesetzblatt and federal administration (bund/bgbl/, bund/fassungen-vor-2019/, bund/verwaltung/)
    From 2023: the binding electronic Bundesgesetzblatt, www.recht.bund.de (official works, § 5 UrhG).
    1949 to 2022: the issue PDFs and their text layer as mirrored by OffeneGesetze (api.offenegesetze.de,
    media.offenegesetze.de; Open Knowledge Foundation Deutschland). This is a copy of the official gazette, used as
    such: every record names its Teil, year, number, page and day of issue, and the official Bundesgesetzblatt is
    binding. OffeneGesetze's software is MIT-licensed; no separate terms for the gazette copies are recorded here.
    Earlier versions of federal acts (bund/fassungen-vor-2019/) are rebuilt from those gazette texts and the
    Fundstellennachweis A (www.recht.bund.de).
    BMF-Schreiben (bund/verwaltung/bmf-schreiben/): Bundesministerium der Finanzen, its own list; official works.

2.3 Länder (laender/)
    Consolidated law imported from Lexifina's earlier repositories, each with its own LICENSE in its folder:
    Bayern (BAYERN.RECHT, Bayerische Staatskanzlei: reuse expressly permitted, commercial or not; the database right
    under §§ 87a ff. UrhG is stated to be unaffected), Sachsen (REVOSax), Brandenburg (BRAVORS), Nordrhein-Westfalen
    (RECHT.NRW), Bremen (Transparenzportal Bremen: its Impressum places the portal's prepared versions under
    CC BY-NC-ND; whether that can reach the words of the laws is left open in laender/bremen/LICENSE) and Hamburg
    (Landesrecht Hamburg, juris for the Land; laender/hamburg/LICENSE and NOTICE.md).
    Added since, from official hosts, as official works:
    - Gazettes: the law gazettes (GVBl., GVOBl.) of Baden-Württemberg, Bayern, Berlin, Brandenburg (also Teil II,
      Amtsblatt, Amtsblatt des MBJS, JMBl.), Hessen, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Niedersachsen, Nordrhein-Westfalen,
      Rheinland-Pfalz, Schleswig-Holstein and Thüringen; the Ministerialblätter of Bayern, Niedersachsen and
      Nordrhein-Westfalen; the Amtsblätter of the Bezirksregierungen in Nordrhein-Westfalen.
    - Bremen: Gesetzblatt and Amtsblatt der Freien Hansestadt Bremen (www.gesetzblatt.bremen.de,
      www.amtsblatt.bremen.de). The sites' notice reserving their texts does not cover the gazettes, which are
      official works under § 5 UrhG (Lexifina's decision of 17 November 2026).
    - Hamburg: the Hamburgisches Gesetz- und Verordnungsblatt from 2014 (laender/hamburg/gvbl/), the issue PDFs as
      published free of charge by the gazette's printer (www.luewu.de). The printer's site excludes commercial use;
      the gazette's text is an official work and is taken as such, official text only (decision of 19 November
      2026). laender/hamburg/LICENSE, written before, says no gazette text is included; this entry supersedes it.
    - Niedersachsen: NI-VORIS (Wolters Kluwer for the Land), the official text of the Land's laws and ordinances, with
      Wolters Kluwer's notes, numbering and abbreviations left out.
    - Verwaltungsvorschriften: REVOSax (Sachsen) and BRAVORS (Brandenburg).
    - Landtag papers and plenary records of every Land except the Saarland. The Abgeordnetenhaus Berlin's PARDOK
      export is licensed Datenlizenz Deutschland - Namensnennung - Version 2.0 (dl-de/by-2-0,
      https://www.govdata.de/dl-de/by-2-0); credit: Abgeordnetenhaus Berlin, PARDOK. The Niedersächsischer Landtag's
      Impressum allows its papers and records to be republished unaltered with the source named. The other Landtage
      state no terms that are recorded here.
    - GovData catalogue records (laender/govdata/, kommunen/<ARS>/govdata/): metadata and links only. Each record
      names the licence its dataset states ("Lizenz:"); most state none.
    Text-and-data-mining reservations (tdm-reservation, § 44b Abs. 3 UrhG) sent by some portals (the juris-run Land
    portals, NI-VORIS, rechtsprechung-im-internet.de) do not reach statutes and decisions, which have no copyright;
    only their official text is taken (Lexifina's decision of 17 November 2026).

2.4 Courts (rechtsprechung/)
    Court decisions and official Leitsätze are official works (§ 5 Abs. 1 UrhG), published anonymised by the courts.
    Federal courts: Lexifina's earlier caselaw repositories (imported), rechtsprechung-im-internet.de (BMJ, juris;
    its Titelzeile, Orientierungssätze and Normenkette are left out), NeuRIS, the Rechtsinformationsportal des Bundes
    (testphase.rechtsinformationen.bund.de, its published bulk snapshot), and the sites of the Bundesarbeitsgericht and
    the Bundesfinanzhof. No further terms are recorded here.
    Länder: BAYERN.RECHT (terms as in 2.3), the Entscheidungsdatenbank der Gerichte in Brandenburg (its terms allow
    decisions to be printed and stored free of charge), NRWE (Justiz Nordrhein-Westfalen), the Land constitutional
    courts' own sites, the Staatsgerichtshof Bremen, and Hamburg's courts (imported; rechtsprechung/hamburg/LICENSE and
    NOTICE.md). No further terms are recorded for NRWE and the constitutional courts.

2.5 Parliament (bundestag/, bundesrat/)
    DIP, Dokumentations- und Informationssystem für Parlamentsmaterialien (search.dip.bundestag.de). Its
    Nutzungsbedingungen (27 February 2023) allow machine access through the API and reuse of the data with the source
    named: "Deutscher Bundestag/Bundesrat – DIP".
    The Bundestag's open data (plenary records, speeches, roll-call votes) and its document server
    (dserver.bundestag.de): no separate terms are recorded here. Drucksachen and Plenarprotokolle are treated as
    official works (§ 5 UrhG); speeches made in public sittings may be reproduced under § 48 Abs. 1 Nr. 2 UrhG. The
    split of records into speeches is machine-made and said so in each file.

2.6 Municipalities (kommunen/)
    Satzungen and Verordnungen of the cities and districts, from their own Ortsrecht collections and Amtsblätter:
    official works under § 5 UrhG.
    Council records from the councils' OParl interfaces and from Munich's RIS (risi.muenchen.de): metadata, agenda
    items, results and resolution texts, with documents linked rather than copied. OParl lets each system state a
    licence; it is not recorded here per record, so each council's own terms apply.
    gemeindeverzeichnis.csv: Statistisches Bundesamt (Destatis), GV-ISys; reproduction and distribution permitted with
    the source named.
    Council records first taken from two re-publishers of council data were withdrawn; their paths are listed in
    data/withdrawn-oparl-aggregator-2026-11-18.txt and data/withdrawn-oparl-muenchen-transparent-2026-11-19.txt.

2.7 Historical constitutions (historisch/verfassungen/)
    The constitutions of 1867, 1871 and 1919 are the de.wikisource transcriptions (status "fertig"), each page tied
    to the scan of the official gazette on Wikimedia Commons; the revisions used are named in each commit. The laws
    themselves are official works long in the public domain. Wikimedia's Terms of Use license text on its projects
    under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/); whatever of that applies to a faithful
    transcription is kept by this credit: Wikisource contributors, de.wikisource.org.
    The Paulskirchenverfassung of 1849 is OCR text of the Reichs-Gesetz-Blatt 1849 as scanned by the Bayerische
    Staatsbibliothek (https://www.digitale-sammlungen.de/view/bsb10518698). The library labels the scan NoC-NC 1.0
    (no copyright; the digitisation for non-commercial use). The law's text is an official work; the scan was used,
    non-commercially, by Lexifina's decision of 18 November 2026. Credit: Bayerische Staatsbibliothek München.

2.8 Private persons
    In gazettes and local law the official text is kept, and a private person's name or home address is replaced by
    "[Name ausgelassen]" or "[Anschrift ausgelassen]". Records essentially about a private person, and in parts
    written before 19 November 2026 records naming one, are kept as metadata only.

3. Questions

A publisher who wants something here changed or removed can open an issue at
https://github.com/lexifina/germany-law/issues.
